summary:
Background: 
There is insufficient data available in Germany on long-term survival after cancer. The Centre for Cancer Registry Data (ZfKD) and the German Cancer Registries (DKR, formerly GEKID) estimate that approximately 4.5 million Germans currently have cancer or have had cancer (prevalence). Optimised and new innovative therapies have continuously improved survival after cancer and led to an increasing number of cancer survivors. This trend will continue in the future. It is therefore becoming increasingly important to systematically record the long-term and late effects of cancer and its treatment and to learn more about the prognosis and health risks of long-term survivors. In Germany, however, there are still major gaps in knowledge about long-term survival after cancer, which are essential for the development of evidence-based care and aftercare concepts.

Research question: 
- Estimate the number of long-term survivors in Germany overall and by type of cancer;
- Describe the composition of today's long-term survivors in terms of underlying disease and treatment; 
- Estimate ten- and fifteen-year survival rates and life expectancy after cancer; 
- Estimate the time to cure (i.e. the time after which the risk of death for a person with cancer no longer exceeds the risk of death for a person of the same age in the general population). 

Methodology:
Applying special statistical methods and models to German Cancer Registry data.
